# Files this repo deliberately does NOT carry from its template.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. Every repo in this tree merges from a `stem` remote. A file
# that a descendant has deleted is not a settled decision as far as git is
# concerned: the next time the template TOUCHES that file, the cascade stops
# with a modify/delete conflict, and whoever is running it has to work out from
# scratch whether the deletion was deliberate. That has already happened
# repeatedly for `sending-indicator.e2e.ts` in bleeps and mandalas, and the only
# record of the intent was a commit message from months earlier.
#
# Listing the path here does not change what git does - the conflict still
# happens - but it makes the answer a lookup instead of an excavation, and
# `scripts/apply-omissions.sh` turns the resolution into one command.
# `web/test/offshoot-omissions.test.ts` fails if any listed path comes back, so
# a cascade that silently reintroduces one cannot be committed quietly.
#
# FORMAT. One path per line, relative to the repo root. `#` starts a comment.
# Give every entry a reason: the next person's question is never "which file",
# it is "why", and an unexplained list is how a deletion outlives the reason for
# it and becomes cargo cult.
#
# THIS FILE USED TO SAY "this template omits nothing - it is where the files come
# from", AND THAT WAS FALSE FROM THE DAY THE GAME LANDED. Files come from
# jolly-roger, not from here: this repo is a descendant with a `stem` remote like
# any other, and it deletes the app demo it inherits because it ships a GAME in
# its place. The cost of the wrong sentence was paid exactly as the header above
# predicts: a cascade stopped on `offline-demo/+page.svelte`, the list said there
# was nothing to look up, and the intent had to be excavated from a merge that
# recorded the deletion in no diff at all. Recorded as a finding on the `work`
# branch, 2026-09-25.

# The app's own greeting demo. This repo replaces it with a game: `routes/play`
# is what a player is here for, and `/demo` would be a second, unrelated thing to
# maintain in a repo whose reference app IS the game. A descendant of THIS
# template deletes `routes/play` in turn and writes its own, which is the same
# move one level down.
web/src/routes/demo

# The same demo, played against a chain in the tab. Deleted for the same reason
# and replaced by `routes/offline`, which plays THIS GAME offline; keeping both
# would mean two offline routes, one of them exercising an app this repo no
# longer has. Note that the two are not interchangeable as examples of the chrome
# decision: the demo keeps the app's chrome and `routes/offline` declares its own,
# because a world that owns the page makes the app's claims false (ADR-0004 on
# the `work` branch, and jolly-roger's ADR-0009).
web/src/routes/offline-demo
